From 08c88a4c0ec796877640a46a8ee2992514563460 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: luhw-dev <luhw@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 01:13:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] feat: handle macOS native window tabs as a single tile
 (#68)

macOS "Merge All Windows" does not collapse the merged windows in the
accessibility tree: every tab stays a separate AXWindow with subrole
AXStandardWindow and a real frame identical to its siblings. AeroSpace's
one AXUIElement = one CGWindowID = one Window leaf assumption therefore turns
N stacked tabs into N independent tiling slots. The tile shrinks to make room
while macOS re-absorbs the new window as a tab, which is what produces the
empty tiles and ghost windows users see.

Model a tab group as ONE tile instead. Exactly one member, the currently
active front tab, is tiled; every other member is parked in a new global
MacosTabbedWindowsContainer, hidden behind the front tab and skipped by
layout, mirroring how minimized and popup windows are already handled.
Switching tabs natively makes a different member the front tab and the next
refresh re-points that same tile to it, preserving the slot so the group does
not jump.

Two invariants keep this safe. Only the front tab is ever framed, because
writing a frame to a background tab tears it out of the group. And the whole
thing is stateless, recomputed from the live tab bar every refresh, so it
tolerates macOS reassigning tab window ids (b56f606 established that they are
not stable) and its worst case is plain tiling.

Detection: frame equality, the classic Amethyst heuristic, turns out to be
unusable in steady state. Two merged tabs share a frame only for the instant
before AeroSpace tiles them; once it writes two different frames the tabs are
torn apart and the signal is gone while the group logically still exists.
Walking for a tab bar does not help either, because only the front tab exposes
one. The one cross-window link that survives is the front tab's bar roster: it
lists one AXTabButton per tab whose label corresponds to a member window's
title. So detection finds windows exposing a multi-tab bar and matches members
by title, requiring a candidate to also be hidden, meaning absent from a fresh
kAXWindowsAttribute read, which is what separates a real background tab from a
separate visible window that merely shares a title (terminals reuse the shell
path). Labels are frequently a decorated form of the title, so matching allows
a prefix or a suffix but never a match in the middle, ranks exact over suffix
over prefix, and assigns each label occurrence to one distinct window.

Cost matters here, because normalizeNativeTabs runs in every refresh and a
refresh is scheduled on every AX notification. A native tab bar lives in the
window's title bar, a shallow child of the AXWindow, never in the content
subtree, so the scan searches breadth-first and stops at the first AXTabGroup.
It is skipped entirely for an app whose windows are all visible, since a group
always has at least one hidden member, and memoized per app on window ids,
hidden bits and titles. Apps are scanned concurrently on their own AX threads
while reconciliation runs in pid order so the tree does not depend on AX
timing, and the whole pass is skipped while the left mouse button is down,
which is when tab tear-out happens.

Everything that can go wrong fails toward plain tiling rather than toward
hiding a real window: a title flicker that drops a member is debounced for one
refresh while the group still exists, released immediately once the group is
gone, a released tab returns to its origin workspace and previous parent kind,
and a same-app window on another workspace is never pulled into a group.

The reconciliation is deliberately split from the AX detection so it can be
unit tested without the live AX path. NativeTabsTest covers group formation,
tab switch preserving the slot, un-merge releasing, the debounce, origin
workspace restoration, duplicate labels, and the over-grouping cases found
against Sequel Ace and Ghostty.

Focus cache: a natively selected background tab is still parked when
updateFocusCache runs, so focusWindow() cannot adopt it. Hold the cache stale
for that one case so the next refresh retries once the tab has been re-tiled.
Every other un-adoptable native focus still advances the cache, otherwise
every refresh would re-attempt setFocus and bounce logical focus back to it.
